This is the direct version. Start a subarray at every index, keep adding values to its sum, and update the answer whenever the sum becomes zero.

A quick dry run for `[1, -1, 2]`:
- Start at index `0`: sums are `1`, then `0`, so the length is `2`.
- Start at index `1`: sums are `-1`, then `1`.
- The result is `2`.

It is easy to verify, but the same values may be added many times, so it is slower than a prefix sum map.

```java
class Solution {
    public int maxLen(int[] arr) {
        int longest = 0;

        for (int start = 0; start < arr.length; start++) {
            long sum = 0;

            for (int end = start; end < arr.length; end++) {
                sum += arr[end];

                if (sum == 0) {
                    longest = Math.max(longest, end - start + 1);
                }
            }
        }

        return longest;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)` apart from the input array.

This uses two passes. First, it creates every prefix sum and sorts a copy of them. The sorted values give each different prefix sum a small integer id. A second pass keeps the earliest index for each id and checks later occurrences.

The important detail is still the earliest position. If the same prefix sum is found at positions `2` and `8`, the subarray length is `8 - 2 = 6`. Replacing position `2` with a later one could only make the answer smaller.

If the array has no zero-sum range, `longest` stays `0`. Using `long` for prefix sums also avoids overflow while values are added.

```java
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.HashMap;

class Solution {
    public int maxLen(int[] arr) {
        int n = arr.length;
        long[] prefix = new long[n + 1];

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            prefix[i + 1] = prefix[i] + arr[i];
        }

        long[] sorted = prefix.clone();
        Arrays.sort(sorted);

        HashMap<Long, Integer> idBySum = new HashMap<>();
        int uniqueCount = 0;

        for (long value : sorted) {
            if (!idBySum.containsKey(value)) {
                idBySum.put(value, uniqueCount++);
            }
        }

        int[] firstIndex = new int[uniqueCount];
        Arrays.fill(firstIndex, -1);
        int longest = 0;

        for (int position = 0; position <= n; position++) {
            int id = idBySum.get(prefix[position]);

            if (firstIndex[id] == -1) {
                firstIndex[id] = position;
            } else {
                longest = Math.max(longest, position - firstIndex[id]);
            }
        }

        return longest;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n log n)` due to sorting the prefix sums.
Space complexity: `O(n)`. Edge cases such as an empty array and an array containing only nonzero-sum ranges are covered.

A zero-sum subarray can be found by comparing prefix sums. This implementation uses an ordered map instead of a hash table. For each prefix sum, only its first position is saved. If it appears again, the distance from that first position is a possible answer.

Here is a small dry run for `[4, -4, 5, -5]`:
- After index `1`, the prefix sum is `0`, giving length `2`.
- After index `3`, the prefix sum is again `0`, giving length `4`.
- The full array is therefore the longest zero-sum subarray.

The ordered map keeps keys sorted, although this is not needed for the answer. Its benefit here is predictable map behavior, at the cost of an extra logarithmic factor.

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <map>
#include <algorithm>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
  public:
    int maxLen(vector<int>& arr) {
        map<long long, int> firstPosition;
        long long sum = 0;
        int answer = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < static_cast<int>(arr.size()); i++) {
            sum += arr[i];

            auto found = firstPosition.find(sum);
            if (found != firstPosition.end()) {
                answer = max(answer, i - found->second);
            } else {
                firstPosition[sum] = i;
            }

            if (sum == 0) {
                answer = max(answer, i + 1);
            }
        }

        return answer;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n log n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)`.

A prefix sum seen at two different positions means the values between those positions add up to zero. The first time a sum appears is important, because keeping that earliest index gives the longest possible range later.

For example, with `[15, -2, 2, -8, 1, 7, 10, 23]`, the prefix sum becomes `15`, then `13`, then `15` again at index `2`. The part from index `1` to `2` has sum zero. Later repeated sums give longer ranges, and the maximum length is found in the same way.

The input array is only read, so it is not changed.

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <algorithm>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
  public:
    int maxLen(vector<int>& arr) {
        unordered_map<long long, int> firstIndex;
        long long prefixSum = 0;
        int best = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < static_cast<int>(arr.size()); i++) {
            prefixSum += arr[i];

            if (prefixSum == 0) {
                best = max(best, i + 1);
            }

            if (firstIndex.find(prefixSum) != firstIndex.end()) {
                best = max(best, i - firstIndex[prefixSum]);
            } else {
                firstIndex[prefixSum] = i;
            }
        }

        return best;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` on average.
Space complexity: `O(n)`.

